= Semantics =

== Overview ==
'''Semantics''' concerns meaning and interpretation.

In language, semantics studies how words, phrases and sentences acquire or express meaning. In logic and computer science, formal semantics assigns mathematically explicit interpretations to expressions in a formal [[Syntax|syntax]].

Semantics is therefore not one single theory. It is a family of questions about how representations connect to what they mean.

== Semantics and syntax ==
'''Syntax''' concerns the form and arrangement of expressions.

'''Semantics''' concerns their interpretation.

Two expressions can be syntactically different while having the same meaning, or syntactically similar while receiving different interpretations.

A formal grammar can determine whether an expression is well formed without determining what it denotes or whether it is true.

== Semantics and pragmatics ==
'''Pragmatics''' concerns how context, speaker intention, social practice and use affect interpretation.

The boundary between semantics and pragmatics is disputed.

For example, understanding "I am here now" depends on contextual information about speaker, place and time. Some theories encode parts of this context into semantics; others treat more of it pragmatically.

== Reference and truth ==
Semantic theories often connect expressions to:

* referents;
* truth conditions;
* semantic values;
* propositions;
* functions;
* possible worlds;
* discourse contexts.

A name can refer to an entity. A predicate can apply to entities. A sentence can be true or false under an interpretation.

Different theories disagree over which of these structures are fundamental.

== Compositionality ==
A central idea in semantics is '''compositionality''': the meaning of a complex expression depends systematically on the meanings of its parts and how those parts are combined.

This helps explain how people can understand sentences they have never encountered before.

Compositionality is influential but requires care because idioms, ambiguity, context and nonliteral language complicate simple part-by-part accounts.

== Lexical and sentence meaning ==
'''Lexical semantics''' studies the meanings of words and lexical items.

Questions include:
* synonymy;
* antonymy;
* polysemy;
* ambiguity;
* hyponymy;
* semantic fields.

'''Sentence semantics''' studies how grammatical structure and lexical meaning combine into sentence meaning.

== Formal semantics ==
In logic and computer science, formal semantics specifies how a formal language is interpreted.

A common structure distinguishes:

<pre>
syntax
→ interpretation
→ semantic value / model / behavior
</pre>

For a logic, semantics may specify which interpretations satisfy a formula.

For a programming language, semantics may specify what a program denotes or how program states change during execution.

For RDF or OWL, semantics defines what graphs or axioms mean and which consequences they entail.

== Model-theoretic semantics ==
A model-theoretic semantics defines interpretations and satisfaction conditions.

For a formula φ and interpretation I:

<pre>
I ⊨ φ
</pre>

means that φ is true/satisfied under I.

Logical consequence can then be defined across all relevant interpretations.

This separates the symbols written in a language from the structures that give those symbols meaning.

== Semantics in knowledge representation ==
[[Knowledge representation]] depends on semantics because machine-readable symbols need defined interpretation if automated reasoning is supposed to preserve meaning.

A relation name such as <code>partOf</code> is only a label until its intended semantics are specified.

Formal systems can additionally declare characteristics such as:
* transitivity;
* symmetry;
* inverse relations;
* subclasshood;
* identity.

These commitments affect valid inference.

== Semantic interoperability ==
Two systems are semantically interoperable when they can exchange information while preserving sufficiently shared meaning.

Matching labels is not enough.

For example, two systems can both use the field "status" while assigning it incompatible meanings.

Ontologies, controlled vocabularies, identifiers and explicit mappings can improve semantic interoperability by making distinctions visible.

== Epistemic notes ==
Semantics does not guarantee truth. A representation can have perfectly precise semantics while describing something false.

Likewise, assigning a URI or machine-readable label does not by itself produce shared human understanding.

